# Persistent object stores

Prior art for the store `research/persistence.dj` describes. The design has a
name — *orthogonal persistence*, a *persistent object store* or *object memory* —
and a long line of implementations, so most of its hard parts have been met
before. Grouped by what each teaches, with the code read where code was
available.

## The direct ancestor: ObjectStore and QuickStore

A 1990s OODBMS pair, and the closest ancestor of "the bytes are the image and a
reference is an offset": persistent pointers "are stored as normal C++ memory
refs — *even on disk*". Reading a page faulted its objects in by mapping the disk
page into the address space and fixing the pointers — *pointer swizzling*. Each
page carried a *meta-object* with two tables: a *mapping object* of every pointer
leaving the page `(virtual address, disk address)`, and a *bitmap object* of where
the pointers sit inside the objects. A page was access-protected until the first
read; the read fetched the mapping object, mapped the pages it named (reusing the
old frame if free), and swizzled. Concurrency was page-level locking; the log was
page diffs.

The mapping object is the outgoing-ref set a compaction pass rewrites, and
swizzle-on-fault is what an offset dereference buys once the base is known.

## libpmemobj

Recorded in `research/persistence.dj`; the piece worth adding is its *publish*,
which makes the single-store publication concrete. Actions are *reserved* without
touching persistent state, populated and persisted, then made visible together:

```c
PMEMoid tail = pmemobj_reserve(pop, &actv[0], sizeof(struct list_node), 0);
...
pmemobj_set_value(pop, &actv[2], &D_RO(root)->head.pool_uuid_lo, head.pool_uuid_lo);
pmemobj_set_value(pop, &actv[3], &D_RO(root)->head.off, head.off);
pmemobj_publish(pop, actv, 4);
```

"The publication is fail-safe atomic in the scope of the entire collection of
actions." Note the types: an OID is a *pool id and an offset*, and the root is a
single OID.

## Boost.Interprocess `offset_ptr`

The offset pointer, as used. Its storage is a union holding the distance from the
pointer's own address to the pointee — `OffsetType m_offset; //Distance between
this object and pointee address` — with a static assert that the offset type is an
unsigned integer of at least `uintptr_t`. So "a reference is an offset" is not a
metaphor: it is a self-relative distance, which is exactly what a relocatable
memory image needs.

## bbolt

Not a graph store, but the mmap mechanics in readable form: `db.mmap(minsz)` maps
the single file, and the mapped size "is 32KB and doubles until it reaches 1GB",
re-mapped as the file grows, over a page-addressed copy-on-write B+tree. Its value
here is the growth policy and the fact that the entire store is one file and one
mapping.

## ZODB

A living object graph. `persistent/cPersistence.c` and `interfaces.py` carry the
object state machine a mapped object store needs: GHOST (-1), UPTODATE (0),
CHANGED (1), STICKY (2), with `_p_oid`, `_p_jar`, `_p_changed`, `_p_serial`,
`_p_state`; "persistence by reachability" from a root; `_p_deactivate` to drop an
object's state (become a ghost) and `_p_invalidate`. Two lessons fall out:

- An object is materialized and dematerialized many times, so a persistent class
  must not have a `__del__`: there "isn't a meaningful relationship between when
  a persistent object's `__del__` gets invoked and any natural aspect of a
  persistent object's life cycle".
- The root is a distinguished OID: "the 8-byte string consisting of 8 NUL bytes …
  is reserved to identify the database root object".

ZODB serializes with pickle, so it is the semantics, not the mechanism.

## Smalltalk images

The purest form of the idea: the whole object memory *is* the file. A reader reads
a header — Spur's is `imageFormat`, `imageBytes`, `startOfMemory`,
`specialObjectsOop` — then walks the heap. A Spur object header is one 64-bit word
carrying a 22-bit class index, a 5-bit format, a slot count and
mark/immutable/pinned bits, and the formats already distinguish pointer, byte,
word, short and compiled-method objects. The validator for those images
(`validate_smalltalk_image`) is the open-time check this project wants:

- errors — `heap.objectOverflow`, `alignment.misaligned`, `oop.invalidPointer`
  ("not immediate, not in heap"), `oop.notObjectStart` ("targets mid-object, not
  a header"), `classTable.missingClass`, `specialObjects.outOfRange`;
- warnings — `format.reserved`, `freeChunk.nonZeroFormat`,
  `reachability.unreachable`;
- always reported — total objects, heap utilization, free chunks, largest object.

That is a whole-heap structural scan with per-object format typing: the model for
the check-at-open, one vertex per node rather than per unit.

## The PM libraries with no public code

- *NV-Heaps* — persistent objects, "specialized pointer types, a memory
  allocator, and atomic sections"; type-safe persistent pointers and
  reference-counted GC.
- *Mnemosyne* — lightweight persistent memory: persistent pointers and
  transactions.
- *Pangolin* — a fault-tolerant persistent *object* library: checksums plus
  parity plus micro-buffering per object, ~1% overhead for GB pools, with online
  detection and recovery. The per-unit-checksum idea, at object granularity.
- *Puddles* (EuroSys 2024) — the closest statement of the goal: PM data should be
  "mappable into a process address space, shareable across processes, shippable
  between machines, consistent after a crash, and accessible to legacy code with
  fast, efficient pointers"; "application-independent recovery", where recovery
  is a property of the data rather than of the programs that access it; and no
  serialize/deserialize to share. Its premise is that mmap load/store alone could
  not deliver all of these "due to conflicting requirements" — the wall
  `persistence.dj` keeps meeting.

Code for these four was not located; they are read as papers only.

## Living and adjacent

- *GemStone/S* — a multiuser, transactional Smalltalk object memory; one shared
  object graph, decades in production.
- *MapDB*, *Chronicle Map* — off-heap, memory-mapped, typed containers shared
  across processes: the mmap-shared mechanics without the graph.
- *CXL object stores* (Clove, cxlalloc) — object-level management of shared
  byte-addressable memory: the hardware future for one world mapped by many
  readers, with its own failure model.

## What to take, and the gap

Take the offset pointer as a self-relative distance (Boost); publish of a
reserved, persisted set of actions as one atomic step (libpmemobj); an object
state machine with an explicit ghost state (ZODB); the whole-heap typed
structural scan as the open-time check (Spur's validator); checksums at object
granularity with parity for recovery (Pangolin); and the mapping-object/bitmap
model of where a unit's references are, which is what a compaction pass rewrites
(ObjectStore).

The gap none of them fills is a **shared, multi-writer, single global graph**. The
OODBMS and PM libraries are single-process; GemStone is multiuser through a
server, not a shared mapping; the shared-map cases are WhiteDB and the Java
off-heap maps, and the forward-looking one is CXL. Several running prompts
mapping one world at once is the part with the least prior art.

